From 15b6e6792547cf195bba6fbfad569ffd613aa985 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PePe Amengual <2208324+jamengual@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:23:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] proxy/pf: redirect traffic the node itself sends to
 ClusterIPs

pf rdr rules only see packets arriving on an interface, so connections the
node itself makes to a ClusterIP (host-network pods, the kubelet, the
apiserver calling webhooks or aggregated APIs) were never redirected: the
packets left through the default route to a Service address that exists
nowhere. FreeBSD 15's pf can translate on output with a filter rule's
rdr-to, so every service anchor now also gets

	pass out quick <af> proto <p> from any to <ClusterIP> port <p> rdr-to <endpoints> port <t> round-robin

after its translation rules (pf rejects an anchor whose rdr/nat rules don't
all precede its filter rules). The main ruleset must reference the anchors
as filter anchors (anchor "cni-rdr/*"), and the reply must be able to reach
the node: the source is the address of the interface the Service CIDR
routes through, so a multi-homed node should route the Service CIDR via its
node-IP interface.

No sticky-address on these rules: pf 15.x records the client's own address
for outbound rdr-to source tracking (fixed by a separate pf patch), which
would send every later connection to the client itself; sessionAffinity is
therefore not honoured for host-network clients.

Also:
- anchors are flushed with -F nat and -F rules ('-F all' would flush the
  global state table);
- stale UDP state cleanup understands outbound redirected states
  ('src -> endpoint (frontend)', killed by their wire key);
- rule writing for a service port moved to writeServiceRules, with a test
  that a NodePort service's filter rule comes after its rdr/nat rules.

Not covered: the host calling a NodePort on its own address (looped
through lo0).
